# Newtype generic parameter names

Domain newtypes in this workspace (`OrderId`, later `AccountId`, `InstrumentId`, …) are thin wrappers. The type-parameter name **must read as the wrapped value**, so call sites stay meaningful when several wrappers share a generic context.

## Rule

- Use a short **uppercase mnemonic of the wrapper / domain noun**, not `T`, `U`, `I`, or `Id`.
- Prefer 2–4 letters that expand to the same words as the struct: `OrderId<OID>`, `AccountId<AID>`, `InstrumentId<IID>`, `CurrencyCode<CC>`.
- Keep that same parameter name in `impl` blocks, bounds, and related functions (`fn new(inner: OID)`).
- If two wrappers appear together, each keeps its own mnemonic (`fn fill<OID, TID>(order: OrderId<OID>, trade: TradeId<TID>)`).

## Examples

```rust
// BAD — the parameter is just "some T"; the wrapper's meaning disappears
pub struct OrderId<T>(T);
impl<T> OrderId<T> {
    pub fn new(inner: T) -> Self { Self(inner) }
}

// GOOD — OID is the order identifier payload
pub struct OrderId<OID>(OID);
impl<OID> OrderId<OID> {
    pub fn new(inner: OID) -> Self { Self(inner) }
}
```

Do not invent a second, unrelated abbreviation for the same wrapper in another module.
